{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 89,
    "dDurationMs": 2611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is a map of the multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 2700,
    "dDurationMs": 6370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or in physics-ese, it’s the maximally extended\nPenrose diagram of a Kerr spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 9070,
    "dDurationMs": 5180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And in english: when you solve Einstein’s\nequations of general relativity for a rotating"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14250,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole, the universe does not come to\nan abrupt halt at the bottom of the gravitational pit"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 21400,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead, a path can be traced out again but\nyou do not end up in the universe that you"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 27100,
    "dDurationMs": 1220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "started in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 28320,
    "dDurationMs": 2900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like I said, it’s a map of the multiverse."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 36110,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to learn some multiverse cartography,\nand to decide whether the strange new regions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 41190,
    "dDurationMs": 5060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "on this map are real, we’re going to need\nto continue a journey that we began a little"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46250,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "while ago when we explored the region just\nabove a rotating black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52210,
    "dDurationMs": 5919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Back then we stopped short of dropping below\nthe event horizon - the boundary of no return."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 58129,
    "dDurationMs": 3430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re going to take that plunge."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 61559,
    "dDurationMs": 6271,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A black hole results when enough mass is concentrated\nin a small enough space that the gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 67830,
    "dDurationMs": 3449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field becomes too strong for even light to\nescape."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71279,
    "dDurationMs": 5130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The inescapable surface surrounding a black\nhole is called the event horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 76409,
    "dDurationMs": 6011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that mass is NOT rotating and does not\nhave any electric charge, the result is a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 82420,
    "dDurationMs": 5939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schwarzschild black hole, which is about as\nsimple as these bizarre objects can get."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 88359,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Add a little spin or charge and things get\neven weirder."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 93259,
    "dDurationMs": 5820,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, we’re going to focus on rotating black holes\nhere, but a lot of this also applies to charged"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 99079,
    "dDurationMs": 2850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black holes, as we’ll see."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 101929,
    "dDurationMs": 5460,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A rotating black hole is described mathematically\nby the Kerr metric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 107389,
    "dDurationMs": 6451,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It describes the way spacetime warps and flows\nin the vicinity of a spinning mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 113840,
    "dDurationMs": 5919,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We sometimes call a rotating black hole with\nno electric charge a Kerr black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 119759,
    "dDurationMs": 4930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Let’s think about what that rotation does\nin familiar terms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124689,
    "dDurationMs": 7461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Think of the worst spinny amusement park rides,\nor an astronaut high-g training machine - you'd"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 132150,
    "dDurationMs": 4169,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "feel yourself flung outwards by centrifugal\nforce."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 136319,
    "dDurationMs": 7611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, in a Kerr black hole that rotation results\nin an outward pressure that partially counteracts"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 143930,
    "dDurationMs": 2449,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inward flow due to gravity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 146379,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This leads to weird effects."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 149239,
    "dDurationMs": 5791,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Above the event horizon is the ergosphere\n- a place where the fabric of space itself"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 155030,
    "dDurationMs": 5629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is whipped into a vortex so fast that not\neven light can resist its motion."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 160659,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The event horizon itself is distorted - wider\nat the equator than the poles, just like the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165959,
    "dDurationMs": 1431,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rotating earth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 167390,
    "dDurationMs": 2819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We saw all that in a previous episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 170209,
    "dDurationMs": 6411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now let’s peek below the event horizon to\nsee how weird things really get."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 176620,
    "dDurationMs": 6569,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we do that, let’s remember how to\nread our multiverse map - our Penrose, or"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183189,
    "dDurationMs": 4701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Carter-Penrose diagram - maybe it’ll help\nus find our way out again."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 187890,
    "dDurationMs": 3260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A couple of things to remember about this\nmap."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 191150,
    "dDurationMs": 6330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Time flows up - for the most part - and one\ndimension of space is left or right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 197480,
    "dDurationMs": 6129,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Light always travels a 45 degree path, and\nnormal objects can only travel paths steeper"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203609,
    "dDurationMs": 1581,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205190,
    "dDurationMs": 5159,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your so-called “future light cone” encompasses\nthe parts of the multiverse you could possibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 210349,
    "dDurationMs": 2991,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "get to at less than light speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 213340,
    "dDurationMs": 5369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your past light cone shows you the parts of\nthe multiverse that you can see, because light"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 218720,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "has had time to reach your position from those\nparts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221560,
    "dDurationMs": 5480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As we take this journey we’re also going\nto plot our path on the Penrose diagram."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 227040,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When we first enter the Kerr black hole, we\nfind it’s just like every non-rotating Schwarzschild"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 232320,
    "dDurationMs": 1600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "black hole we’ve ever visited."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233920,
    "dDurationMs": 5120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Down becomes the future, because falling space\ndrags us there faster than the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 239040,
    "dDurationMs": 3320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Space and time switch places."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 242360,
    "dDurationMs": 4599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In a regular black hole we’d be crushed\nby the central singularity pretty quickly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 246959,
    "dDurationMs": 6881,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But now as we fall the outward pressure due\nto the black hole’s rotation starts to win"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 253840,
    "dDurationMs": 2369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "against that inward flow."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256209,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In fact the flow eventually slows to less\nthan the speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 260739,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This leads to a second event horizon called\nthe inner horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265210,
    "dDurationMs": 6190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now, some pretty terrible things happen here,\nand you don’t survive the crossing."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 271400,
    "dDurationMs": 4759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But we’re going to pretend that you’re\nDeadpool or something you get across intact-ish"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 276159,
    "dDurationMs": 2921,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- what do you see?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279080,
    "dDurationMs": 4339,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other side, space and time switch back to\ntheir proper order."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 283419,
    "dDurationMs": 6661,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’re now free to not fall, so you can\njet around and explore the interior."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 290080,
    "dDurationMs": 4880,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Below you is the black hole singularity - but\nit looks odd."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 294960,
    "dDurationMs": 5239,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The singular, central point has been spun\nout into a ring of infinite density."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300199,
    "dDurationMs": 6030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s surrounded by a second ergosphere,\nwhere again the rotational flow of space exceeds"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 306229,
    "dDurationMs": 1720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "light speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 307949,
    "dDurationMs": 1940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what next?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 309889,
    "dDurationMs": 4341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "First probably take a ride in the ergosphere,\nbecause why not?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 314230,
    "dDurationMs": 4430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When you’re dizzy enough you can try to\nturn around and go back the way you came through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 318660,
    "dDurationMs": 5360,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the inner horizon, or you can plunge through\nthe singularity ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 324020,
    "dDurationMs": 3670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In either case, you’re in for a weird time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 327690,
    "dDurationMs": 5490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To understand what happens here, we have to\nthink like a general relativist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333180,
    "dDurationMs": 6199,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In order to create these maps of spacetime,\nphysicists use the equations of general relativity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 339379,
    "dDurationMs": 7091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to trace what we call geodesics - these are\nthe paths that objects take when not experiencing"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 346470,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "any force."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347470,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, the path a ball takes if I drop\nor throw it - after it leaves my hand."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 353449,
    "dDurationMs": 4900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A nice way to map the gravitational field\nis according to the geodesics of objects in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 358349,
    "dDurationMs": 4290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "freefall that start motionless relative to\nthe gravitational body."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 362639,
    "dDurationMs": 5310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For example, if you drop a trillion balls\nfrom space they’ll trace the downward cascade"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367949,
    "dDurationMs": 5801,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of space - and that flow would be pretty spherically\nsymmetric."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373750,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Same with the interior of a Schwarzschild\nblack hole - every ball, every geodesic reaches"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 379500,
    "dDurationMs": 4530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the same point and, very importantly, ends\nthere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384030,
    "dDurationMs": 6180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But within a Kerr black hole, the singularity\nis a ring, and the trillion balls would fall"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 390210,
    "dDurationMs": 3420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "towards the disk bounded by that ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 393630,
    "dDurationMs": 7120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Only the ones exactly on the equator hit the\nring and, those geodesics do end - same infinite"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400750,
    "dDurationMs": 2879,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "squish as in a Schwarzschild black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 403629,
    "dDurationMs": 6521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But those that approach the disk experience\nan overwhelming anti-gravitational force from"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 410150,
    "dDurationMs": 1609,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the spinning singularity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 411759,
    "dDurationMs": 6611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Our balls, and the geodesics they follow,\nrebound and travel back out."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418370,
    "dDurationMs": 4720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we take that outward ride, let’s\npush a little harder and try to get through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 423090,
    "dDurationMs": 1190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 424280,
    "dDurationMs": 5039,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With a lot of speed - and I mean a LOT - it’s\npossible to overcome the anti-gravitational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 429319,
    "dDurationMs": 3521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field in the middle of the ring and punch\nthrough."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 432840,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you don’t find yourself on the other\nside of the same region."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 437030,
    "dDurationMs": 7670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The ring is like a portal to a new, very different\nregion of spacetime - and that’s because"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 444700,
    "dDurationMs": 6301,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the geodesics passing through one side do\nnot map to those passing through the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 451001,
    "dDurationMs": 3418,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Through the portal things get very weird."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 454419,
    "dDurationMs": 5701,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, the ring singularity becomes\nentirely repulsive - as though it had negative"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 460120,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "mass."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461120,
    "dDurationMs": 6639,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Also, there are no horizons above you - there’s\na straight path to the outside universe - but"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 467759,
    "dDurationMs": 2440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it is certainly not our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 470199,
    "dDurationMs": 5591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For one thing, the lack of event horizons\nabove you means the ring is a naked singularity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 475800,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "as well as being repulsive."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 478320,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Surrounding the ring on this side is a toroidal\nregion in which it’s possible to accelerate"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 485130,
    "dDurationMs": 1560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to infinite speed."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 486690,
    "dDurationMs": 6800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And as some of you probably remember - faster\nthan light travel means the possibility of time travel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 493490,
    "dDurationMs": 6810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are trajectories in this torus that\nlead you back to your starting location - in both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500300,
    "dDurationMs": 2019,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "space AND time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 502319,
    "dDurationMs": 2030,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So-called closed-timelike curves."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504349,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This is the Carter time machine, after the\naussie physicist Brandon Carter who did much"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 509979,
    "dDurationMs": 4361,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of the early exploration of the Kerr spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 514340,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This time travel and naked singularity stuff\nis good reason to think this part of the mathematical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 520480,
    "dDurationMs": 3300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "structure of the Kerr black hole is NOT real."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523780,
    "dDurationMs": 6560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We’ll come back to how it can be avoided\nin the math - for now, let’s whip once around"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 530340,
    "dDurationMs": 4570,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "back to our own past and hop back through\nthe ring."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534910,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At this point we can follow an outgoing geodesic\nacross the inner horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 540640,
    "dDurationMs": 2390,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "However you’re in for a surprise."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 543030,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That outgoing flow continues above the inner\nhorizon, and will eventually eject you past"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 548870,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the outer horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550480,
    "dDurationMs": 4350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That’s a little confusing because black\nholes are supposed to be inescapable."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 554830,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So what’s with this conveyor belt of space\ntaking you out of the black hole?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559380,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Don’t you have the same conveyor belt pulling\nyou in over that same region?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 564350,
    "dDurationMs": 7190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well you sort of cross the same space, but\nnot through the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 571540,
    "dDurationMs": 2490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The black hole is in your past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 574030,
    "dDurationMs": 5730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You’d need to travel back in time-slash-faster\nthan light to get back there."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 579760,
    "dDurationMs": 5750,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Extending the Kerr metric from ring singularity\ninto the future leads to something else."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585510,
    "dDurationMs": 3930,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It leads to what looks like a white hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589440,
    "dDurationMs": 6320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now we saw that in the Schwarzschild solution,\na white hole exists in the past of a purely"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 595760,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hypothetical eternal non-rotating black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 600010,
    "dDurationMs": 5350,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Kerr black hole also has a white hole\nin the past, but it has one in the future"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 605360,
    "dDurationMs": 1160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "too."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 606520,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that’s where the outgoing geodesics\ngo, carrying you with them."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611190,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But not only is the black hole in your past\n- the entire universe that you came from is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 617180,
    "dDurationMs": 1400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in your past."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 618580,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You caught a glimpse of the entire history\nof that universe the moment you crossed into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 623340,
    "dDurationMs": 1860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the white hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 625200,
    "dDurationMs": 5940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That white hole will eject you as forcibly\nas the black hole pulled you down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 631140,
    "dDurationMs": 2420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But eject you where?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633560,
    "dDurationMs": 4020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well this has to be a new universe, because it’s\ndefinitely not the one you came from."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637580,
    "dDurationMs": 6680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s not the bizarro negative time traveling universe either\n- here, the laws of physics are the same as"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 644260,
    "dDurationMs": 5450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "where you started - at least as far as general\nrelativity is concerned."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 649710,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you try to head back to the white hole,\nyou won’t find it - you’ll only find a"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 655840,
    "dDurationMs": 2990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "new black hole that lies in its future."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658830,
    "dDurationMs": 6470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you enter that you’ll be able to\ntake the entire fair ground ride once again,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665300,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "skipping ahead to yet another universe, and\nso on ad infinitum."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 670600,
    "dDurationMs": 5170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This may all sound fun, but unfortunately\nthere’s the inconvenient fact of your utter"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 675770,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "obliteration before you ever cross the inner\nevent horizon in the first place."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 681210,
    "dDurationMs": 4790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The complete Penrose diagram for the Kerr\nspacetime has not one, but two inner event"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 686000,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "horizons leading to two parallel wormholes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 690300,
    "dDurationMs": 6040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You reach one if you embrace your fall and\nhead down, the other if you try to resist"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 696340,
    "dDurationMs": 1990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and exit the black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698330,
    "dDurationMs": 4640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These correspond to the region below the event\nhorizon but they’re causally disconnected,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 702970,
    "dDurationMs": 6160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the difference being that time flows\nforward if you go in one direction and backwards"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 709130,
    "dDurationMs": 1300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the other."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710430,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So you have this messed up situation where\nthe direction of the flow of time actually"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 715470,
    "dDurationMs": 2780,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "clashes at the inner horizon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 718250,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This would be okay-ish if the black hole was\nempty, but it’s not okay if there’s even"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 723970,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the tiniest bit of matter or radiation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 726490,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If there’s any stuff at all in the black\nhole then the forward flow of time carries"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 731360,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a current of positive energy, while the backwards\ncarries negative energy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 736980,
    "dDurationMs": 6340,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When they meet they don’t cancel out - instead\nthey stream past each other producing enormous pressure."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743320,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That pressure produces its own gravitational\neffect on par with the black hole itself,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749570,
    "dDurationMs": 1840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "accelerating the streams further."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 751410,
    "dDurationMs": 3050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is an exponential runaway effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 754460,
    "dDurationMs": 7150,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So if you do get to the inner horizon you are\nin a bath of energy on par with the Big Bang."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 761610,
    "dDurationMs": 1130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Good luck with that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 762740,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Besides, this so-called mass inflation means the inner structure of the Kerr black"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 768010,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "hole is catastrophically unstable, and the\nwhole thing collapses, shutting off any potential"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773510,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magical portals to time machines or new universes\n- probably before they ever exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 778510,
    "dDurationMs": 6130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This whole counter-streaming instability thing\nwas figured out by Roger Penrose in the context"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 784640,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of charged, non-rotating black holes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788220,
    "dDurationMs": 5700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But it almost certainly applies to Kerr black\nholes also because the two are very similar."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 793920,
    "dDurationMs": 6490,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In electrically charged, or Reissner-Nordström\nblack holes the electromagnetic field within"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 800410,
    "dDurationMs": 6100,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "causes massive tension, or negative pressure\nthat produces an antigravitational effect."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 806510,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That resists the inward flow just like rotation\ndoes in a Kerr black hole."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810950,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have the same inner horizon and chain\nof wormhole-connected universes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 815910,
    "dDurationMs": 5530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They just don’t have the ring singularity\nleading to the bizarro-verse, so Kerr is more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821440,
    "dDurationMs": 2210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "fun than Reissner-Nordström."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 823650,
    "dDurationMs": 1180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So there you have it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 824830,
    "dDurationMs": 4860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Now you know everything you need in order\nto travel the multiverse and travel through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 829690,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 830690,
    "dDurationMs": 4280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Just a little wishful thinking, a rotating\nblack hole, and don’t forget to slip your"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 834970,
    "dDurationMs": 2090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "map in your back pocket."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837060,
    "dDurationMs": 8840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Never travel the multiverse without a Carter-Penrose\ndiagram of the maximally extended Kerr spacetime."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845900,
    "dDurationMs": 4730,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last time we talked about the greatest failed\nexperiment in the history of physics - the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 850630,
    "dDurationMs": 5510,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Michelson-Morley experiment, and how it did\naway with the idea of the luminiferous aether,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 856140,
    "dDurationMs": 4890,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and so doing paved the way for the acceptance\nof Einstein's relativity."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 861030,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Your comments were relatively fantastic."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 865470,
    "dDurationMs": 5720,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Elliot Heath wonders if we are now in a similar\nposition to the end of the 19th century now"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 871190,
    "dDurationMs": 2200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- in that we might be on the cusp of massive\nchange."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 873390,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well, unlike 120 years ago, I wouldn't say\nphysicists today are under any illusions that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 878670,
    "dDurationMs": 1610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "physics is \"complete\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 880280,
    "dDurationMs": 4400,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They're very aware that there are big gaps\nin their understanding."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884680,
    "dDurationMs": 5270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And I'd say most HOPE for massive change,\nbecause that's probably whats needed to find a way"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 889950,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "forward."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890950,
    "dDurationMs": 4960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Will that next step be as surprising as relativity\nand the quantum revolution?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895910,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I personally think it will be - whatever points\nbeyond the current mess may need to be revolutionary"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 902150,
    "dDurationMs": 6260,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and come from an unexpected direction - otherwise\nsurely we'd have seen it before."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 908410,
    "dDurationMs": 4600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Zatar123 asks whether the Michelson Morley\nexperiment has been performed at much higher"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 913010,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "velocities to get a better reading - say 10%\nthe speed of light."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 917670,
    "dDurationMs": 1520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well the answer is essentially no."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 919190,
    "dDurationMs": 6220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the solar system is moving at 368 km/s relative\nto the rest-frame of the cosmic microwave"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925410,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "background radiation - and that's as close as you can\ndefine to an absolute definition of \"stillness\"."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 931390,
    "dDurationMs": 5770,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So to do better you'd need a device traveling\nfaster than that - essentially impossible"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937160,
    "dDurationMs": 3290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "even for a spacecraft, at least currently."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 940450,
    "dDurationMs": 5000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But the experiment has been performed with\nmuch higher precision using other methods"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 945450,
    "dDurationMs": 5990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- lasers, vacuum chambers, strange quantum\nmechanical states of light - you name it."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 951440,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "No indication has ever been found that the\nspeed of light depends on the speed of the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 955560,
    "dDurationMs": 2970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "device or of the observer."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 958530,
    "dDurationMs": 3110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you took issue at my bad math."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 961640,
    "dDurationMs": 3270,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I said infinity times zero is zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964910,
    "dDurationMs": 2430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Indeed that isn't generally true."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 967340,
    "dDurationMs": 5840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was being a bit lazy - what I wanted to\nget across was the following: As you rewind"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 973180,
    "dDurationMs": 5910,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the universe towards zero age, distant\npoints in space end up closer and closer - and there"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 979090,
    "dDurationMs": 6240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is no point that is so distant that it won't\nbe arbitrarily close to you at some point in the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 985330,
    "dDurationMs": 2140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "rewind BEFORE time gets to zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 987480,
    "dDurationMs": 6520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So in an infinite universe, every definable\ngalaxy or definable location ends up right here on"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 994000,
    "dDurationMs": 2640,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "top of us at some point in the rewind."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 996650,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The universe approaches infinite density in\nthe limit as time approaches zero."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1001970,
    "dDurationMs": 3540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you were right to be annoyed at my lazy\nmath language."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1005510,
    "dDurationMs": 6900,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paolo Sena tells us that Quintessence is literally\n“quinta essentia” or fifth element in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1012410,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Latin."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1013410,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Paulo, I did not know that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1014940,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thank you- that's some erudite nerdery right\nthere."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1018520,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And we had plenty of the other types of nerdery\nalso - I mentioned the math nerds already,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1022960,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "but I was especially excited to see plenty of\nroleplaying game nerds talking about Sons"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1029100,
    "dDurationMs": 3819,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of Ether and accusing me of being part of\nthe magic technocracy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1032919,
    "dDurationMs": 5960,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Well I always thought of myself as more order of\nhermese, or maybe hufflepuff, but I defer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1038879,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to the wisdom of the sorting hat that is the\nyoutube comment section."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 1043320,
    "dDurationMs": 1760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Nerds."
    } ]
  } ]
}
